WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.189
Film Courage: So then (if I understand this
right) that FINAL DESTINATION was the first

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:05.010
screenplay that you actually sold?

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.680
Jeffrey Reddick: That was the first one I
sold.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.230
I wrote one and it never got made but I was
actually hired to write a sequel for a movie

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:16.530
called PUMPKINHEAD, which is one of my favorites
and they are remaking it now and so hopefully

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:23.250
I can use my sequel idea for the sequel of
the remake but before I got an agent, I got

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:29.630
hired because Brad Krivoy was the producer
and he was taking pitch meetings and I wrote

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:33.440
a pitch that he liked and he basically hired
me and another writer to finish the rest of

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.190
the script and then the company got bought
by somebody and all of this so neither one

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:43.070
of our scripts ever got made but no, that
was my first paying job as a writer (which

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.190
was fun).

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.550
That was a little crazy because you are like
“Wow!

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.410
I am writing something that I grew up loving.”

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:55.940
That was the first thing that I actually sold
and I’d written like 5 or 6 or 7 scripts

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.050
before A FINAL DESTINATION that never got…and
rightly so for some of them when you go back

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.460
and read them, it’s like “Whoah!

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.720
These are pretty bad.”

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.329
But there’s a couple of nuggets of ideas
that I might turn into something some day.

00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:12.399
Film Courage: I’m just wondering how did
it happen?

00:01:12.399 --> 00:01:13.829
What were the details?

00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:17.009
So you are working at New Line Cinema they
had known you for years…

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.490
Jeffrey Reddick: They had known me for years.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.549
I was trying to get a TV agent and then my
friend Mark Callen was like “Oh, this would

00:01:21.549 --> 00:01:25.319
make a great idea for a feature.”

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:28.549
Back in the day you could actually sell a
treatment to a studio.

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:32.439
You didn’t have to write a whole script
and because I worked at the studio, I knew

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:40.479
how when the winds would change very frequently
so something would be hot one week and not

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:41.639
the next week.

00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:44.529
I was like “Let me write a treatment, because
it’s a great concept.”

00:01:44.529 --> 00:01:48.689
And some friends of mine worked for a producer
outside of the studio that had to deal with

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:52.450
the studio, so I’m like let me go work with
him and their producer partner and bring the

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.630
idea back to the studio because I knew that
would give me a better chance of getting that

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.249
set up (it was with the producer attached).

00:01:58.249 --> 00:02:04.459
I had the treatment and then they were all
grown ups and then SCREAM came out (and I

00:02:04.459 --> 00:02:06.950
love SCREAM) and Kevin Williams is a dear
friend of mine.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.450
And the minute that came out it was like “Let’s
make them all teenagers.”

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:13.659
I had to go rewrite the treatment to make
them all teenagers and then the studio just

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:14.980
couldn’t get their head around certain aspects.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.049
They were like “It’s great.

00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:19.980
It’s cool but how can you make certain things
happen.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:20.980
It just doesn’t make any sense?”

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.500
And I’m like “That’s the whole point
to having a horror film.”

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.040
So the studio just kept passing on it and
finally the producers (it was Warren Zaid

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.250
and Craig Perry) they were like “Well, if
you pass on it again, we’re going to go

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.269
to Dimension” which was kind of New Line’s
rival, Miramax at the time.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:38.430
And they were like “We’ll buy it!”

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.590
Film Courage: You said the magic word.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.629
Jeffrey Reddick: So then they bought the treatment
and then they hired me to write the first

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:48.709
draft of the script and then we went out to
directors after that.

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:49.709
It was a pretty quick process.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.970
We sold the original story in ’97 and the
movie came out in 2000, so in Hollywood time

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.939
that’s pretty, pretty fast.

00:02:56.939 --> 00:03:00.650
Film Courage: When you did the casting for
it, how did you find the lead?

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.959
Jeffrey Reddick: Actually New Line did all
the casting through there casting department.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:08.489
I’d put my list together of my dream cast
at the time.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:10.609
But I love the cast that we ended up with.

00:03:10.609 --> 00:03:14.349
And it’s funny because I’m friends with
Devon (Sawa), he’s directing now and he’s

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:19.590
got a new TV show so I produced his first
short that he did and he’s a really talented

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.590
guy.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.000
We’ve actually reconnected in the last few
years and he’s just a great guy.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.370
It is funny because when I was writing the
movie I was kind of (you know) trying to turn

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.560
some of the tropes on their heads and so there
are very few movies that have a final guy

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:32.879
in it, it’s always a final girl.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:36.349
So I wanted to write a movie with a final
guy in it.

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:42.459
And his character I think definitely stands
out and he’s such a great actor, too.

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:44.440
He did such a great job and he was CASPER,
too.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:49.349
It’s always funny making fun of him because
he was in CASPER floating around in with Christina

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:52.049
Ricci when they were kids, dancing ridiculous.

00:03:52.049 --> 00:03:54.439
Devon, if you’re watching this, ridiculous.

00:03:54.439 --> 00:04:00.390
Film Courage: I would see him in so many things
because I used to go into Blockbuster Video

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:04.480
all the time and I just remember “There
he was again!”

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.480
[on a DVD cover].

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.829
Jeffrey Reddick: Yeah, we was really hot then,
too.

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:11.780
I think he…ah man…there is something North?

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:17.590
I’m blanking on the name of it…there were
some coyotes and people in the wilderness.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.989
I’m trying to think of the name of it, I’ll
try to look the movie up…but it was a really

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:21.989
big movie.

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:26.130
It was CASPER and then that one, he did IDLE
HANDS…I think IDLE HANDS…I don’t know

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.889
if that was pre or post FINAL DESTINATION…I
think it might have been point FINAL DESTINATION.

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:35.620
But yeah, he was definitely on everybody’s
like ones-to-watch-list.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.090
And we shot it in Canada so was got a lot
of Canadian talent, as well.

